!> everything has been accumulated.  The module is therefore in two layers, and
!> a caller runs them in that order:
!>
!>  1. ACCUMULATE, at the PD level.  `*_term_eval` (spatial), `*_nuclear_eval`
!>     (one retained nuclear index, per atom), `*_tangent_eval` (one contracted
!>     nuclear index, aggregate) and `*_hvp_eval` (one retained + one contracted,
!>     per atom).  Every one of these is an accumulator: zero the buffers once
!>     per evaluation point, then call for every active atom and every active
!>     pair.  Nuclear signs are already applied.
!>
!>  2. LIFT, to the level set.  `cfc_spatial_eval`, `cfc_tangent_eval` and
!>     `cfc_hvp_eval` consume those accumulators and return derivatives of S
!>     itself.  The first two are O(1) per evaluation point; the third is O(1)
!>     per atom.  No chain rule is left for the caller.
!>
!> The pair term does not factorize -- the (1 - vec(s_a).vec(s_b))**4 factor
!> couples the two centers -- so the pair loop of layer 1 is irreducibly O(n**2)
!> in *work*.  Contracting the nuclear index inside the kernel is what keeps it
!> O(n) in *storage*: the pairwise nuclear Hessian is never formed.
!>
!> Every routine dispatches on `max_deriv` so a low-order request never
